3.  **Direct Attack on a Core Problem:** For math and coding, there is usually a single correct path or answer. Models that spread their probability mass across many tokens at each step are essentially "hedging their bets" or "being confused." Forcing the model to commit to a single, high-confidence path is a very direct and intuitive way to improve performance on these tasks.
